data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"input_json_delta","partial_json":"{\"kind\": \"decision\", \"title\": \"Store shopping carts in Postgres or Redis?\", \"body\": \"Carts currently live in a 4 KB session cookie, so long carts get truncated. Moving them server-side means choosing a store.\\n\\n**Postgres:** carts are durable and transactional, and they can be queried (abandoned-cart emails, analytics) with no new infrastructure. Writes add load to the main database, and expired carts need a cleanup job. Reads are a little slower than Redis.\\n\\n**Redis:** fast reads and writes, with TTLs that expire abandoned carts automatically. It adds a new service to run and secure. Carts are lost unless persistence is configured, and eviction settings can silently drop them.\\n\\nRecommendation: Postgres, unless cart traffic is high enough to strain the primary database. The durability and queryability matter more for a shop than a few milliseconds of latency.\", \"actions\": [{\"label\":\"Use Postgres\",\"kind\":\"resolve\",\"recommended\":true},{\"label\":\"Use Redis\",\"kind\":\"resolve\"}]}"}}

