data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Postgres vs. Redis for carts:** Postgres keeps carts durable and transactional, can be queried for things like abandoned-cart emails, and needs no new service, but writes add load to your main database and expired carts need a cleanup job. Redis is fast and its TTLs expire abandoned carts automatically, but it adds a service to run and secure, and carts are lost unless you configure persistence and eviction carefully. I recommend Postgres unless cart traffic is high enough to strain the primary database.\n\nThe choice is yours, so I recorded it as decision **A1** (\"Store shopping carts in Postgres or Redis?\") with a \"Use Postgres\" button (recommended) and a \"Use Redis\" button. I didn't read or write any files."}}

